  "script": {
    "segments": [
      {
        "narration": "One Dead Sea Scroll is a treasure map on metal.",
        "visual_query": "judean desert cliffs caves",
        "visual_fallback": "desert cave"
      },
      {
        "narration": "It came out of Cave 3 at Qumran in March 1952, and it was wrong from the start: copper, not parchment, and corroded so hard it could not be unrolled.",
        "visual_query": "qumran cave entrance desert",
        "visual_fallback": "cliff cave"
      },
      {
        "narration": "So a Manchester professor sawed it into twenty-three strips instead. The strips turned out to be a list \u2014 sixty-four hiding places, most of them near Jericho.",
        "visual_query": "ancient corroded copper sheet",
        "visual_fallback": "corroded metal"
      },
      {
        "narration": "One entry alone names nine hundred talents of silver. That is somewhere near thirty tons. So where is it?",
        "visual_query": "dead sea salt shore",
        "visual_fallback": "dead sea"
      },
      {
        "narration": "Nowhere. Expeditions from 1962 on have come back empty, and the leading reading now is that this was temple wealth catalogued in a panic \u2014 a list written for a burial that may never have happened.",
        "visual_query": "judean wilderness jericho hills",
        "visual_fallback": "desert hills"
      }
    ],
